WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.672
Every object above absolute zero emits infrared radiation, but a camera estimates apparent temperature from the radiation reaching its detector.

00:00:08.672 --> 00:00:14.520
The result depends on the surface, environment, sensor, distance, atmosphere, angle, and settings.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:20.368
Thermal contrast is the apparent temperature difference between a feature and its surroundings.

00:00:20.368 --> 00:00:24.200
Strong contrast can make patterns easy to see.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:30.452
Weak contrast can hide a real condition even when the sensor is operating correctly.

00:00:30.452 --> 00:00:36.300
Emissivity describes, at a high level, how effectively a surface emits thermal radiation.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:44.568
High-emissivity materials often provide more dependable apparent-temperature readings than shiny, low-emissivity surfaces, which can reflect radiation from other objects.

00:00:44.568 --> 00:00:47.190
Reflections can create false impressions.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:55.458
Polished metal, glass, water, and other surfaces may show heat from the sky, sun, operator, equipment, or nearby objects.

00:00:55.458 --> 00:01:01.307
The hottest-looking area in a palette may not be the hottest physical object.

00:01:01.307 --> 00:01:05.945
Sensor resolution determines how many detector elements describe the scene.

00:01:05.945 --> 00:01:12.600
Small targets at long distance may occupy too few pixels for reliable measurement or identification.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.835
Digital enlargement cannot recreate detail the detector never captured.

00:01:16.835 --> 00:01:21.070
Measurement often needs more target pixels than simple detection.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:28.532
A tiny feature may appear as a colored point while its reading is blended with the background.

00:01:28.532 --> 00:01:33.170
Distance and focus therefore affect both recognition and reported value.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.808
Palettes and display ranges map values to colors or shades.

00:01:37.808 --> 00:01:42.447
Changing them can emphasize contrast without changing the underlying data.

00:01:42.447 --> 00:01:51.522
Analysts should examine the scale, range, settings, and original file rather than treating red as automatically dangerous or blue as safe.
